WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.712
Offline reinforcement learning in domains like
healthcare and education must estimate

00:00:05.792 --> 00:00:09.448
the environment's transition dynamics from fixed
batch data,

00:00:09.528 --> 00:00:14.989
yet standard maximum likelihood estimates give high
variance policies that make dangerous

00:00:15.069 --> 00:00:16.856
mistakes where data is sparse.

00:00:16.936 --> 00:00:21.588
This paper introduces Bayesian Inverse Transition
Learning, a gradient free,

00:00:21.668 --> 00:00:27.316
constraint based method that uses an expert's near
optimal demonstrations to clip a Bayesian

00:00:27.396 --> 00:00:29.744
posterior over the transition dynamics.

00:00:29.824 --> 00:00:33.604
The result is a distribution of dynamics that
guarantees safe,

00:00:33.684 --> 00:00:35.161
high performing policies,

00:00:35.241 --> 00:00:40.888
recovering one hundred percent accuracy on states
where the best action is known and cutting

00:00:40.968 --> 00:00:43.690
policy variance dramatically across datasets.

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:48.167
In fields like healthcare and education we cannot
experiment freely,

00:00:48.247 --> 00:00:53.633
so offline reinforcement learning must learn the
environment's transition dynamics purely

00:00:53.713 --> 00:00:56.274
from a fixed batch of collected experience.

00:00:56.354 --> 00:01:01.618
The trouble is that this data only covers the
actions the original users actually took,

00:01:01.698 --> 00:01:04.689
leaving much of the state and action space unseen.

00:01:04.769 --> 00:01:09.909
Standard maximum likelihood estimates of the
dynamics therefore produce policies that

00:01:09.989 --> 00:01:15.192
swing wildly from dataset to dataset and can
recommend genuinely unsafe actions in the

00:01:15.272 --> 00:01:16.850
regions where data is thin.

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:21.169
The key insight is that the people who generate
this offline data,

00:01:21.249 --> 00:01:24.458
such as clinicians, are usually acting near optimally.

00:01:24.538 --> 00:01:28.721
Their choices quietly encode which actions are good
and which are bad,

00:01:28.801 --> 00:01:33.592
yet a plain maximum likelihood fit of the dynamics
throws that information away.

00:01:33.672 --> 00:01:40.596
Earlier gradient based inverse reinforcement
learning methods try to recover an expert's belief about the dynamics,

00:01:40.676 --> 00:01:45.894
but they never connect their estimate back to the
true environment and they suffer from

00:01:45.974 --> 00:01:48.269
the fragility of gradient optimization.

00:01:48.349 --> 00:01:52.410
We want to exploit the expert signal directly and
without gradients.

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:55.754
This work introduces Inverse Transition Learning,

00:01:55.834 --> 00:01:58.611
a gradient free and constraint based approach.

00:01:58.691 --> 00:02:04.450
It converts a near optimal expert policy into a set
of constraints on the transition dynamics,

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:09.916
and then clips a Bayesian posterior over the
dynamics so that every sampled model yields

00:02:09.996 --> 00:02:12.028
a safe and high performing policy.

00:02:12.108 --> 00:02:13.581
Beyond the method itself,

00:02:13.661 --> 00:02:18.985
the paper carefully analyzes when and why maximum
likelihood estimation of the dynamics

00:02:19.065 --> 00:02:21.407
breaks down under uneven data coverage,

00:02:21.487 --> 00:02:27.060
and it shows how combining the constraints with
uncertainty produces an informative ranking

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:30.290
of actions in the states where the expert is unsure.

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:35.142
Given the rewards, an epsilon optimal expert
policy, and the batch data,

00:02:35.222 --> 00:02:40.579
we use the closed form Bellman equations to write
constraints that demand the value of

00:02:40.659 --> 00:02:45.321
actions the expert takes exceed the value of
actions the expert never took.

00:02:45.401 --> 00:02:51.643
Any dynamics satisfying these constraints recovers
what we call the expert's epsilon ball structure.

00:02:51.723 --> 00:02:57.523
We then place a Dirichlet multinomial posterior
over the dynamics and use rejection sampling,

00:02:57.603 --> 00:03:00.937
keeping only the samples that satisfy the constraints.

00:03:01.017 --> 00:03:06.248
The surviving samples form a clipped posterior that
respects the expert's knowledge.

00:03:06.328 --> 00:03:11.622
Separate constraint sets handle a fully optimal
expert and a partially uncertain one,

00:03:11.702 --> 00:03:18.640
with a tunable slack term that enforces the
structure even where action non linearity would otherwise break it.

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:24.599
All experiments run on a synthetic tabular Markov
decision process with fifteen states

00:03:24.679 --> 00:03:27.099
plus a terminal state and six actions,

00:03:27.179 --> 00:03:30.258
using a discount factor of zero point nine five.

00:03:30.338 --> 00:03:32.956
The true dynamics are deliberately mixed,

00:03:33.036 --> 00:03:37.298
sometimes uniform and sometimes highly skewed
toward a few states,

00:03:37.378 --> 00:03:39.601
to create rich and varied behavior.

00:03:39.681 --> 00:03:41.575
We study two coverage regimes,

00:03:41.655 --> 00:03:46.839
a low data setting of fifteen episodes and a high
data setting of three hundred,

00:03:46.919 --> 00:03:51.511
crossed with three levels of expert optimality that
create zero, three,

00:03:51.591 --> 00:03:53.617
and six uncertain policy states.

00:03:53.697 --> 00:03:59.210
Every reported number is averaged over one thousand
independently generated datasets.

00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:03.600
The clipped posterior dominates both maximum
likelihood and the un clipped Bayesian posterior.

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:07.690
It guarantees one hundred percent accuracy on
states where the expert knows the single best action,

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:11.780
in every data and optimality setting,

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:15.870
whereas maximum likelihood ranges from sixty seven
to ninety two percent.

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:19.960
It also never selects an action outside the
expert's epsilon ball,

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:24.050
so it makes no truly bad mistakes.

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:28.140
Strikingly,

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:32.230
even though the constraints say nothing explicit
about which action to pick in uncertain states,

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:36.320
our method is still more accurate there than the
baselines,

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:40.410
which means the constraints implicitly transfer the
expert's uncertainty structure.

00:04:40.790 --> 00:04:46.092
A central finding of the analysis is that more data
does not rescue maximum likelihood.

00:04:46.172 --> 00:04:49.680
Even in the high data regime it keeps making bad mistakes,

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:52.278
choosing actions outside the epsilon ball,

00:04:52.358 --> 00:04:57.660
because expert only data leaves large parts of the
state and action space unexplored no

00:04:57.740 --> 00:04:59.887
matter how many episodes we collect.

00:04:59.967 --> 00:05:05.454
Sweeping the optimality level from zero through
four confirms that our method holds across

00:05:05.534 --> 00:05:07.248
degrees of expert optimality,

00:05:07.328 --> 00:05:12.630
recovering policies that can even beat the expert
while keeping variance close to zero.

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:14.949
The numbers tell a clear story.

00:05:15.029 --> 00:05:16.772
With a fully optimal expert,

00:05:16.852 --> 00:05:21.331
our method reaches a Q star metric of exactly zero
with zero variance,

00:05:21.411 --> 00:05:23.024
in both low and high data,

00:05:23.104 --> 00:05:30.382
compared to fifty nine point seven five for maximum
likelihood and one hundred forty two for the plain posterior.

00:05:30.462 --> 00:05:34.224
At the hardest setting, epsilon equals four with
high data,

00:05:34.304 --> 00:05:36.308
we score eight point seven nine,

00:05:36.388 --> 00:05:42.495
beating maximum likelihood's twenty point one one
and roughly matching the near optimal expert.

00:05:42.575 --> 00:05:46.858
Accuracy on deterministic states is a constant one
hundred percent,

00:05:46.938 --> 00:05:53.370
and the policy variance collapses from a standard
deviation near fifty two down to essentially zero.

00:05:53.750 --> 00:06:00.581
By clipping a Bayesian posterior over the
transition dynamics with constraints derived from a near optimal expert,

00:06:00.661 --> 00:06:04.521
you obtain gradient free offline policies that are
provably safe,

00:06:04.601 --> 00:06:07.492
can outperform the expert who generated the data,

00:06:07.572 --> 00:06:11.251
and carry dramatically lower variance than maximum
likelihood.

00:06:11.331 --> 00:06:17.616
The same recipe of constraints plus uncertainty
also yields a ranking of actions in the uncertain states,

00:06:17.696 --> 00:06:23.860
making the learned policies more informative for
high stakes planning such as clinical decision making.
